  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study uses neuron-level analysis and editing to mitigate gender bias, but it does not test any of the listed versions about pole-level interpretability, explanation fidelity, or attribution graphs.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports neuron-editing and task-performance results, but does not measure how many neurons have one or two jobs, test explanations on held-out activations, score automated explanations, or build attribution graphs. The rows below record relevant neuron-editing outcomes; they do not test a listed version.",
    "test_reason": "The paper locates and edits neurons to mitigate gender bias in five 7–8B decoder LLMs and reports bias and task metrics. It never audits what share of neurons have one or two jobs. It does not score explanations on held-out activations, run simulation-based automated scoring, build neuron attribution graphs, or study vision models. Its neuron interpretations (unembedding projections of a few case-study neurons) are a tool for its editing method, not a test of any version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Broadly correct. The authors also stress two further points. Masking just two shallow 'general neurons' sharply degrades general capabilities by altering higher-layer neuron coefficients. INE, they say, outperforms the fine-tuning and other editing baselines in balancing bias reduction against capability preservation.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study identifies and edits neurons for gender-bias mitigation; it does not conduct a pole-level job audit or score neuron explanations on held-out activations.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The paper reports that editing the two selected early-layer general neurons sharply reduces task accuracy, whereas editing the INE-selected neurons produces the task results in Tables 4–5 and 11–13.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "In Llama-7B, the early-layer 'general neurons' activated by the <start> token are described as lacking interpretability under unembedding projection, yet masking two of them collapses RACE (63.5→31.5) and arithmetic (51.9→7.5) accuracy.",
        "proposed_claim": "Some causally critical early-layer neurons in LLMs have no readable unembedding-space interpretation, and ablating them broadly disrupts capabilities.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R1",
          "R2",
          "R3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Interpretable Neuron Editing on five 7–8B LLMs modestly reduces gender-bias metrics while keeping common-task accuracy roughly unchanged.",
        "proposed_claim": "Editing a small, causally filtered set of LLM neurons can reduce a targeted bias with little capability loss.",
